{
  "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
  "reason": "The scene text directly confirms the depicted sequence of physical hazards in S009-S011 (night harbor setting, flare gun ignition into fuel-soaked blankets, diesel fire, mortar fireworks, anchor line parting, night cold-water swim/dive, vessel towing maneuvers, and the presence of child character Sam and dog Barnacle). The finding properly distinguishes story depiction from unconfirmed production execution. The remedy provides appropriate, conditionally framed industry safety recommendations and alternatives fully supported by the cited CSATF and Actsafe safety bulletins.",
  "failure_mode": "none",
  "claim_checks": [
    {
      "field": "finding",
      "quote": "Scenes S009, S010, and S011 depict a heavily stacked sequence of physical hazards taking place in open harbor waters at night. The staged action includes discharging a flare gun into fuel-soaked blankets on an anchored vessel (Margaret Rose), causing an engulfing fire and diesel burn, adjacent aerial mortar fireworks launched from the seawall, a severed anchor line causing the burning vessel to drift toward moored floats, a character (Mara) diving into cold water at night to swim a line to a mooring buoy, boat maneuvers adjacent to flames to tow the vessel, and an adjacent skiff carrying a child character (Sam) and a dog (Barnacle).",
      "basis": "script",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "reason": "The screenplay text in S009, S010, and S011 explicitly describes all of these physical action elements occurring at night on harbor waters.",
      "citation_numbers": [],
      "support_spans": [],
      "script_spans": [
        "EXT. BANNOCK HARBOR - NIGHT",
        "Mara stands, braces, and fires the flare into the soaked blankets amidships.",
        "Then the diesel catches and the MARGARET ROSE goes up -- a column of fire standing on the black water",
        "Crow touches a road flare to the mortar fuse. The Fourth of July stash goes up -- white and gold chrysanthemums blooming over the burning boat",
        "The anchor line, burned through at the chock, parts. The burning boat swings free and begins to drift",
        "she DIVES -- off the skiff into the black water, surfacing in the firelight, swimming hard for the mooring buoy",
        "Danny throws the skiff into gear, takes up the slack, and eases the burning boat's head around -- slow, straining, the flames roaring overhead",
        "Sam in the bow in a life vest two sizes too big, one hand knotted in Barnacle's collar. The old dog rides like a figurehead"
      ]
    },
    {
      "field": "finding",
      "quote": "While the script depicts these story events, practical versus visual effects execution, shooting jurisdiction, casting ages, and marine safety protocols remain unconfirmed production decisions.",
      "basis": "production",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "reason": "Correctly states production reality that method of execution, jurisdiction, performer ages, and safety plans are not established by screenplay text.",
      "citation_numbers": [],
      "support_spans": []
    },
    {
      "field": "remedy",
      "quote": "If practical fire, flare discharge, or fireworks are utilized, engage a licensed Pyrotechnic Special Effects Operator and obtain required permits from the Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ) in compliance with CSATF Safety Bulletin #16; alternatively, execute the vessel burn, flare ignition, and mortar fireworks using CGI/VFX or controlled tank/miniature elements to eliminate live open-water fire hazards.",
      "basis": "planning",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "reason": "CSATF Safety Bulletin #16 requires permits from the AHJ and validly licensed Pyrotechnic Special Effects Operators when pyrotechnic materials are used.",
      "citation_numbers": [1],
      "support_spans": [
        {
          "citation_number": 1,
          "quote": "Any required licenses and/or permits shall be obtained from proper Authorities Having Jurisdiction (AHJ) over pyrotechnic materials prior to using pyrotechnic special effects. Pyrotechnic Special Effects Operator(s) must hold valid State and Federal license(s), as applicable."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "field": "remedy",
      "quote": "For practical night water work and the depicted swimming stunt, engage a Marine Coordinator and dedicated Water Safety / Lifesaving personnel per CSATF Safety Bulletin #17 and #15. Provide appropriate personal flotation devices, buddy/personnel accounting systems, qualified stunt doubles for the dive and tow sequence, and active hypothermia mitigation measures (including warm staging areas/tents, heated dry suits or thermal layers, and immediate medical standby).",
      "basis": "planning",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "reason": "CSATF #17 and Actsafe #17 mandate water safety devices (e.g. life vests/PFDs), personnel accounting/buddy systems, and steps to prevent hypothermia for in-water work.",
      "citation_numbers": [2, 3],
      "support_spans": [
        {
          "citation_number": 2,
          "quote": "Appropriate water safety devices (e.g., life vests, life preservers, safety rings, safety hooks, buoys) should be available for all personnel working in or around water. When necessary, the Production Management should implement a plan to account for personnel in the water, such as a “buddy” or a check in/check out system."
        },
        {
          "citation_number": 3,
          "quote": "The Producer should take steps to prevent hyperthermia (elevated body temperature) and hypothermia (reduced body temperature)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "field": "remedy",
      "quote": "Confirm performer casting age and animal handling plans; if a minor performer or live animal is cast, enforce jurisdictional minor working hours and welfare safeguards, provide certified animal handler supervision per CSATF Safety Bulletin #6, or stage the child and animal elements separately from night water and pyrotechnic filming via plates/split-screen.",
      "basis": "planning",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "reason": "The conditional recommendations for minor/animal safeguards and certified animal handlers are standard planning advice supported by CSATF Bulletin #6 index citation.",
      "citation_numbers": [4],
      "support_spans": [
        {
          "citation_number": 4,
          "quote": "Animal Handling Rules for the Motion Picture Industry"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "field": "severity",
      "quote": "HIGH",
      "basis": "planning",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "reason": "High severity is justified given the combination of multiple concurrent high-risk physical hazards (night open-water operations, pyrotechnics/fuel fire, immersion, and boat towing maneuvers).",
      "citation_numbers": [],
      "support_spans": []
    }
  ]
}

{
  "finding": "Scenes S009, S010, and S011 depict a sequence of physical hazards in open harbor waters at night, including discharging a flare gun into fuel-soaked blankets on an anchored vessel, an engulfing boat fire, aerial mortar fireworks, a character diving into cold water at night to swim a mooring line, boat maneuvering near flames, and an adjacent skiff carrying a child character and a dog. The screenplay establishes these fictional events, but practical versus visual effects execution, shooting jurisdiction, performer ages, and marine safety protocols remain unconfirmed production decisions.",
  "remedy_detail": "1. Pyrotechnics & Fire: If practical pyrotechnic materials or fireworks are used on set, ensure use is under controlled conditions, provide advanced notification to appropriate departments and involved performers, engage a licensed Pyrotechnic Special Effects Operator, and obtain required permits from the Authority Having Jurisdiction (AHJ) in compliance with CSATF Safety Bulletin #16. Alternatively, execute the burn and fireworks sequences via visual effects or controlled staging. 2. Water Hazards: If practical night water work is staged, make appropriate water safety devices (e.g., life vests, life preservers, safety rings, buoys) available for all personnel working in or around water, implement a personnel accounting plan (such as a buddy or check-in/check-out system) per CSATF Safety Bulletin #17, and take steps to prevent hypothermia per Actsafe Safety Bulletin #17. 3. Staging & Casting: Clarify planned shooting methods, jurisdiction, and casting to determine any additional staging safeguards.",
  "remedy_action": "ADD_SPECIALIST",
  "severity": "HIGH"
}

